  "content": [
    {
      "id": "introduction",
      "title": "Introduction",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "You have already seen a regular queue elements go in, elements come out in order. A monotonic queue adds&nbsp;<strong>one extra rule</strong>&nbsp;on top of that.",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "1-what-does-monotonic-mean",
      "title": "1. What Does Monotonic Mean?",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Monotonic simply means&nbsp;<strong>always increasing</strong>&nbsp;or&nbsp;<strong>always decreasing</strong>. No random order. The queue maintains a sorted property at all times.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "items": [],
          "url": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785247649967-638339540.png",
          "caption": "",
          "content": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785247649967-638339540.png",
          "text": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785247649967-638339540.png",
          "alt": "Increasing vs decreasing vs zig-zag sequences"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
            "Monotonic means the values move in only one direction. They either keep going up (increasing) or keep going down (decreasing).",
            "In a <b>monotonic</b> increasing sequence, each next value is greater than or equal to the previous one. It never goes down.",
            "In a <b>monotonic</b> decreasing sequence, each next value is smaller than or equal to the previous one. It never goes up.",
            "If the <b>values</b> go up and down randomly (zig-zag), then it is not monotonic."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785247652028-843700007.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\">",
          "items": [],
          "content": null
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "text": "<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">Easy way to remember:&nbsp;</span><strong style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">No up-and-down mix = monotonic</strong><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">&nbsp;</span>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "2-why-does-this-exist",
      "title": "2. Why Does This Exist?",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Consider this problem find the&nbsp;<strong>maximum element in every window of size K</strong>&nbsp;as it slides across an array.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<b>Step 1:</b> When 8 comes, we remove 4 and 3 because they are smaller and useless now. The deque becomes [8] and the maximum is 8.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785247654063-380400191.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\">",
          "items": [],
          "content": null
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<b><br></b>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<b>Step 2:</b> When 2 comes, it is smaller than 8 so we keep both. The deque becomes [8, 2] and the maximum is still 8.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785247655999-722782261.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\">",
          "items": [],
          "content": null
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<b><br></b>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<b>Step 3: </b>When 6 comes, we remove 2 because it is smaller than 6, but keep 8. The deque becomes [8, 6] and the maximum is still 8.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785247657540-573663627.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\">",
          "items": [],
          "content": null
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<b><br></b>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<b>Step 4:</b> When the window moves, 8 goes out of range so we remove it. The deque becomes [6, 5] and the new maximum is 6.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785247658994-806255090.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\">",
          "items": [],
          "content": null
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Brute force scan every window of size K for its maximum.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Every time the window moves, we check all K elements inside it to find the maximum. This takes O(K) work for one window.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Since there are about O(n) windows in total, the overall time becomes O(n × K), which is slow for large K.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "For large arrays and large K this is too slow.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A monotonic queue solves this in&nbsp;<strong>O(n)</strong>&nbsp;every element is added and removed at most once.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "3-the-core-idea",
      "title": "3. The Core Idea",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The key insight is this if a&nbsp;<strong>smaller element</strong>&nbsp;enters the window after a&nbsp;<strong>larger element</strong>, the smaller one can&nbsp;<strong>never</strong>&nbsp;be the maximum while the larger one is still in the window.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "So throw it out immediately. It is useless.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "items": [],
          "url": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785247660515-1792593.png",
          "caption": "",
          "content": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785247660515-1792593.png",
          "text": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785247660515-1792593.png",
          "alt": "Smaller element discarded immediately after a larger one"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "This is a&nbsp;<strong>monotonic decreasing queue</strong>&nbsp;front is always the largest.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "4-step-by-step-walkthrough",
      "title": "4. Step by Step Walkthrough",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785247662070-741792018.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\">",
          "items": [],
          "content": null
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">At&nbsp;</span><strong style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">i = 0 (value = 4)</strong><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">, the deque is empty so we add index 0. The deque becomes [0] (value [4]). The window is not full yet.</span>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785247663885-217859213.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\">",
          "items": [],
          "content": null
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "At&nbsp;<strong>i = 1 (value = 3)</strong>, we compare with the last element (4). Since 4 is greater, we keep it and add index 1. The deque becomes [0, 1] (values [4, 3]). The window is still not full.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785247666339-494878525.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\">",
          "items": [],
          "content": null
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "At&nbsp;<strong>i = 2 (value = 8)</strong>, we remove smaller elements from the back. First 3 is removed, then 4 is removed because both are smaller than 8. We add index 2, so deque becomes [2] (value [8]). Now the window is full, and the maximum is 8.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785247668764-579670413.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\">",
          "items": [],
          "content": null
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">At&nbsp;</span><strong style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">i = 3 (value = 2)</strong><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">, 8 is greater than 2 so we keep it and add index 3. The deque becomes [2, 3] (values [8, 2]). Index 2 is still inside the window, so max remains 8.</span><br>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\"><img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785247670822-969084987.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\"></span>",
          "items": [],
          "content": null
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">At&nbsp;</span><strong style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">i = 4 (value = 6)</strong><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">, we remove 2 because it is smaller than 6. We keep 8 since it is larger, then add index 4. The deque becomes [2, 4] (values [8, 6]). Index 2 is still valid, so max is 8.</span><br>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\"><img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785247672758-702349171.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\"></span>",
          "items": [],
          "content": null
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "At&nbsp;<strong>i = 5 (value = 5)</strong>, 6 is greater than 5 so we keep it and add index 5. The deque becomes [2, 4, 5] (values [8, 6, 5]). Now index 2 goes out of the window, so we remove it. The deque becomes [4, 5] (values [6, 5]), and the new maximum is 6.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "items": [],
          "url": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785247675356-861545276.png",
          "caption": "",
          "content": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785247675356-861545276.png",
          "text": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785247675356-861545276.png",
          "alt": "Deque state after i = 5 and final result"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Final result:<code>[8, 8, 8, 6]</code>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "items": [],
          "codes": {
            "javascript": "public int[] slidingWindowMax(int[] arr, int k) {\n    int n = arr.length;\n    int[] result = new int[n - k + 1];\n    Deque<Integer> dq = new ArrayDeque<>();   // stores indices\n\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n\n        // remove front if it is outside the current window\n        if (!dq.isEmpty() && dq.peekFirst() < i - k + 1)\n            dq.pollFirst();\n\n        // remove from back all elements smaller than current\n        // they can never be maximum while current is in window\n        while (!dq.isEmpty() && arr[dq.peekLast()] < arr[i])\n            dq.pollLast();\n\n        dq.offerLast(i);   // add current index\n\n        // window is full — record the maximum\n        if (i >= k - 1)\n            result[i - k + 1] = arr[dq.peekFirst()];\n    }\n\n    return result;\n}"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "5-two-types-of-monotonic-queue",
      "title": "5. Two Types of Monotonic Queue",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Depending on the problem, you use one of two variants:",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading4",
          "text": "1. Monotonic Decreasing Queue",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785247677283-105991537.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\">",
          "items": [],
          "content": null
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">A monotonic decreasing queue keeps elements in </span><b style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">decreasing order</b><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">, so the front always has the largest element.&nbsp;</span><br>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "text": "When a larger element comes, all smaller elements at the back are removed, and it is mainly used for sliding window maximum.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading4",
          "text": "2. Monotonic Increasing Queue",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785247679076-478789537.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\">",
          "items": [],
          "content": null
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">A monotonic increasing queue keeps elements in </span><b style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">increasing order</b><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">, so the front always has the smallest element.&nbsp;</span><br>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "text": "When a smaller element comes, all larger elements at the back are removed, and it is mainly used for sliding window minimum.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading3",
          "text": "Complexity",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "text": "",
          "items": [],
          "content": "{\"headers\":[\"Property\",\"Value\"],\"rows\":[[\"Time complexity\",\"O(n)\"],[\"Space complexity\",\"O(k)\"],[\"Underlying structure\",\"Deque\"],[\"Decreasing queue gives\",\"Window maximum\"],[\"Increasing queue gives\",\"Window minimum\"]]}"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">Each element is added to the deque only once and removed at most once, so the total work is linear.&nbsp;</span><br>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "That’s why the overall time complexity is O(n), and the space complexity is O(k) since the deque never holds more than k elements at a time.&nbsp;",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">Compared to the brute force O(n × k), this approach is much faster for large inputs.</span><br>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "6-when-to-use-it",
      "title": "6. When to Use It",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "You should think about a monotonic queue whenever a problem asks for the maximum or minimum in every window of size k.&nbsp;",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Use a decreasing queue for maximum and an increasing queue for minimum.&nbsp;",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "If the problem talks about next greater or smaller elements, then a monotonic stack is used, which follows the same core idea of removing useless elements and keeping only the important ones.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
